<div style="border: 2px solid #1a5276; padding: 10px; text-align: center; background-color: #e0f2f7;">
    <h1 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Look at the Traditional and Modern Building Materials. – Lesson # 4</h1>
    <p style="color: #1a5276; margin: 5px 0 0 0;"><em>Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</em></p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 4</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Writing</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 7th Grade (CEFR: A2.2)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> The Town Square</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> Look at the Traditional and Modern Building Materials.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <b>Specific Objective:</b> Writing: Productive: Write descriptive reviews about content or products. Interactive: Write information for a presentation with peers in a coherent manner.
        </td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <b>Learning Outcome:</b> Writing: Can describe with peers features of the town square for a class presentation (e.g., "Next to the market is the Town Council."). Can write a short descriptive review of a town square attraction, highlighting personal impressions and details (e.g., "The town square was lively and colorful. The market is next to the tall building in the middle of the town, and I enjoyed trying the local food."). Can help organize information about the town square for a presentation by describing its key features and surroundings (e.g., "The town square has a central plaza with a fountain, and there are shops and restaurants around it.").
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 30%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 20%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays a large picture of a town square, showing both traditional and modern buildings. Students are asked to verbally identify familiar places (e.g., "There is a park," "I see a tall building.") and objects (e.g., "fountain," "statue"). The teacher then points to various elements and elicits descriptive adjectives (e.g., "Is this building traditional or modern?").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models writing short descriptive sentences about the displayed town square picture. For example, the teacher writes on the board: "This is a busy town square. There is a historic church next to a modern building. A big fountain is in the plaza." The teacher explicitly uses "there is/are" and prepositions of place while writing, emphasizing clear sentence structure for description. The teacher also models pronunciation for words like "street," practicing the /s/ sound.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Am I telling you to talk or to write about the town square right now?" (Expected answer: "Write.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"When I write 'There is a park,' what does 'There is' tell us?" (Expected answer: "Where something is/exists.")</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5-7 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "The Town Square".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> The teacher distributes a short descriptive text (a simple review or description) about a famous town square, including a simple map or illustration. The text uses target vocabulary (e.g., "historic building," "modern plaza," "fountain," "market," "street," "tradition") and grammar (e.g., "There is/are," prepositions like "next to," "across from"). For example: "The Plaza Mayor is a busy town square. There is a historic statue in the middle. Next to it, you can find a market. This market is famous for its traditional food. There are also many modern shops across from the market. A spacious park is nearby."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> The student can describe in writing the location and features of places using simple, connected sentences and basic prepositions.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b> Students read the text silently.</p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist questions:</b> "What kind of place is Plaza Mayor?" "Is it an old place or a new place, or both?" Students write short answers.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information matching:</b> Students match phrases describing locations from the text to numbered points on the provided map/illustration (e.g., "Match 'historic statue' to point A on the map"). They also identify sentences that use "traditional" or "modern" to describe materials or styles.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">8-10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Writing</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students practice writing sentences using "There is/are," prepositions of place (e.g., near, between, across from, next to), demonstratives (this, that, these, those), descriptive adjectives (historic, modern, busy, quiet, tall), and sequencing words (First, Second).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: decimal; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Controlled Practice (Cloze description):</b> The teacher provides a paragraph describing a different town square with blanks for students to fill in. Blanks will target "there is/are," prepositions of place, key vocabulary (e.g., fountain, market, building), and adjectives. Example: "___ a large plaza in the middle. The _____ building is ____ the library. You can see a ______ (traditional/modern) roof. A big _______ is ______ the park."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Semi-controlled Practice (Sentence Builders):</b> Students are given a list of nouns (e.g., 'statue', 'park', 'market', 'town hall'), adjectives (e.g., 'historic', 'modern', 'spacious'), and prepositions. They must write 3-4 descriptive sentences about a hypothetical town square, combining these words. Example: "Write a sentence using 'town hall', 'next to', and 'modern'." (e.g., "There is a modern town hall next to the market.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pair Interaction (Describing a map in writing):</b> In pairs, students receive a simple map of a town square with 3-4 labeled landmarks (e.g., 'library', 'school', 'fountain', 'crosswalk'). Students take turns writing sentences describing the location of these landmarks relative to each other for their partner to check. For example, Student A writes: "The school is across from the library. There is a crosswalk near the school." Student B checks for correct use of grammar and vocabulary. This activity also integrates the pronunciation practice of /s/ in words like "school," "street," "spacious" when students read their written sentences aloud to their partner during checking.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">15-18 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "The Town Square".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students will work in small groups (3-4 students) to *write a short descriptive review* of a town square, either a real one they know or an imagined one based on provided pictures. The review should highlight at least two attractions, describe their features, and mention personal impressions, connecting to the theme of traditional and modern building materials. They will organize this information coherently for a potential class presentation.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Collaborative Written Review.</p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Each group selects a picture of a town square (provided by teacher, showing diverse architectural styles).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">They discuss and collectively draft a descriptive review (5-7 sentences) including:
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">General description (e.g., "This town square is busy and lively.").</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Location descriptions using "There is/are" and prepositions (e.g., "There is a historic monument in the center. Next to it, you can find a modern library.").</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Descriptions of features using adjectives (e.g., "The roof of the town hall is traditional. These buildings are tall.").</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Personal impressions (e.g., "I enjoyed visiting this spacious plaza.").</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">They use sequencing words to organize their descriptions (e.g., "First, we see... Second, there is...").</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students are encouraged to use the target vocabulary and grammar structures. The teacher circulates, offering support and prompting for more descriptive language and correct syntax.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10-12 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Peer Assessment using a simple checklist.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Each group exchanges their written descriptive review with another group. Students use a provided checklist to assess the other group's writing.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Peer Checklist):</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Did the review describe at least two features of the town square? (Yes/No)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Did the review use "There is/are" correctly at least twice? (Yes/No)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Did the review use at least two prepositions of place correctly? (Yes/No)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Did the review use at least two descriptive adjectives (e.g., historic, modern, busy)? (Yes/No)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Is the writing clear and easy to understand? (Yes/Needs work)</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete two sentences on a small slip of paper:</p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Today I learned to write about..."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"One new word I used today is..."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What part of writing the town square description was easy for you?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was challenging, and how did you try to solve it?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> The collaborative writing and peer assessment worked well to reinforce grammar and vocabulary for descriptive writing. Students successfully used "There is/are" and prepositions. Some groups needed more scaffolding with sequencing words and integrating personal impressions smoothly into the review. Next time, I will provide more explicit sentence starters for expressing feelings or opinions within the descriptive review.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> In the next lesson (Mediation & Integration), students will consolidate the descriptions they wrote today. They will use their written reviews to prepare and deliver a short presentation to the class, possibly explaining their favorite parts of the town square or comparing traditional vs. modern elements, integrating all four skills.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">2-3 min</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>